ntMetadata":{"context_description":"Recent geopolitical tensions in the Middle East, particularly involving Iran and oil supply risks, have pushed the 10-year Treasury yield to around 4.69-4.72% as of August 11, 2026, near its 2026 highs amid sticky inflation readings and elevated term premiums. Traders are monitoring how persistent energy price spikes and fiscal pressures interact with Federal Reserve policy, where markets price roughly even odds of near-term rate hikes versus holds. Key upcoming catalysts include the August CPI release and subsequent FOMC communications, which could shift rate path expectations and influence whether yields test levels above 4.75% or 5% before year-end. Historical precedents show yields can move quickly on combined inflation and supply shocks, though recession risks remain a moderating factor.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-11T22:46:46.558Z"},"version":"v1"}]
